News that the world`s two largest futures exchanges will set aside their differences to join in a single after-hours electronic trading system has left some players wondering where they stand.
Even spokesmen for the Chicago Board of Trade and the Chicago Mercantile Exchange said it wasn`t clear how merger plans would affect partner exchanges and vendors.
So far, other exchanges and vendors say they still consider themselves to be in the game, though they aren`t sure what the game is.
The Merc`s system, called Globex, was scheduled to begin operating this fall. Three other exchanges-the New York Mercantile Exchange, the MATIF exchange in Paris and the Sydney Futures Exchange-have signed letters of intent to list some of their futures contracts on Globex.
The Board of Trade decided last fall to come up with its own system rather than join Globex. The Board of Trade`s alternative, called Aurora, was unveiled in March.
Friday`s announcement that the exchanges would strive for ''a single, unified after-hours trading and order delivery system'' brought a wave of enthusiasm from potential users who didn`t want to buy two systems.
But by Tuesday, the enthusiasm had been converted to questions, although there was no disagreement that a unified system was the logical path. At stake are agreements the two exchanges have made with technology vendors to design and build the separate systems, as well as with the other exchanges that had planned to trade their contracts on one of the two systems.
The New York Mercantile Exchange views its pledge to join Globex as remaining in effect. But Nymex Chairman V. Lou Guttman says he doesn`t know whether the final system will be Globex, or even resemble it, or whether the Globex timetable remains in effect.
