It has taken months of negotiations, but local clients of the collapsed derivatives trader MF Global could be about to see some of their money again.
A court ruling has paved the way for the release of more than $200 million to traders whose accounts were frozen when the company went bankrupt late last year after it lost some high stakes bets on Europe's debt crisis.
But no one is counting their money just yet - former clients, including many self-funded retirees, worry that any payout could be delayed by more court action.
MF Global was one of the world's biggest traders of financial contracts known as derivatives, and was run by former Goldman Sachs chairman Jon Corzine.
It went under late last year, after making the wrong bet on a $6 billion-plus trade against troubled European nations.
In Australia, around $310 million from 11,000 clients was frozen.
Securities dealer Ross Anderson is a former MF Global Australia client who set up a support group for traders.
"Guys working on oil rigs who had their own superannuation invested in things, we've had self-funded retirees who were active in the markets trying to generate their own returns, lots and lots of farmers who were hedging their wool crops or their wheat crops," he said.
"Thousands and thousands of ordinary Australians have been caught up in this."
Now, following a ruling this week by Justice Ashley Black in the New South Wales Supreme Court, traders could get some of their money back before the end of the year.
Chris Campbell from accountancy firm, Deloitte, is the liquidator of MF Global Australia.
"It's not a blanket return for all clients because, as Justice Black has ruled, it is to be done across the four product pools for each client," he said.
"Based on the funds that I have recovered already, and the estimate of the claims, the return to clients is ranging from just under 70 cents in the dollar through to, in one category, 100 cents in the dollar."
However, Mr Campbell says it will still take a while for investors to see any of their money returned.
"I estimate that that'll take between two and three months to start paying the money out," he added.
"There's approximately $310 million worth of claims, and we are currently holding about $236 million in cash."
Deloitte is also seeking around $50 million from the liquidators of the UK arm of MF Global and the US parent company.
Former clients say the liquidation process has taken too long, and they would have preferred getting they money back sooner even if it meant a smaller return.
"There's been no distribution to clients at all. Deloitte was appointed administrator on the first of November last year, and still no distribution," he said.
"In the US, people were paid, I think, 75 per cent of their money before December last year."
Chris Campbell says the liquidation process in Australia can be lengthy.
